[2026-01-29T23:34:53.625Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017X8c7bMdSPpB7NntVQi9Wi","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a comprehensive view of the Report Tabs system. Let me create a detailed analysis report.\n\n## Comprehensive Analysis of Report Tabs System\n\n### 1. Tab Architecture and Navigation Patterns\n\nThe Report Tabs system consists of 6 primary tabs organized around research intelligence analysis:\n\n**Tab Structure:**\n- **OverviewTab** - Dashboard view with key metrics and summaries\n- **FindingsTab** - Detailed findings with expandable cards and filtering\n- **SourcesTab** - Source credibility analysis with tabular presentation\n- **EntitiesTab** - Named entity extraction grouped by type\n- **AnalysisTab** - Contradictions, gaps, and causal chains\n- **PerspectivesTab** - Multi-perspective analytical views\n\n**Navigation Pattern:**\nAll tabs are exported from a centralized index file (`src/components/report/tabs/index.tsx`) which suggests a tabbed interface that likely switches between these components. The tabs appear to be designed for sequential navigation through research results, moving from high-level overview to detailed analysis.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Data Presentation Approaches\n\n**By Tab:**\n\n**OverviewTab:**\n- **Grid-based stats**: 5-column grid displaying key metrics (Findings, Avg Confidence, Sources, Perspectives, Red Flags)\n- **Metric cards** with color-coding and optional child visualizations\n- **Aggregated data**: Confidence distribution, warning summaries, key insights\n- **Limiting pattern**: Shows top 6 insights, 5 recommendations, 3 gaps (avoiding information overload)\n\n**FindingsTab:**\n- **Expandable cards**: Header shows summary with type/confidence badges; body reveals full content\n- **Hierarchical filtering**: Search, type filter, confidence filter (high/medium/low)\n- **Rich metadata display**: Temporal context, event date, extracted data, supporting sources\n- **Visual hierarchy**: Uses chevron icons and transitions for expand/collapse behavior\n\n**SourcesTab:**\n- **Credibility distribution bar**: Visual breakdown of source quality tiers\n- **Tabular layout**: Rows with source title, type badge, credibility progress bar, and date\n- **Hover states**: Light background change for interactivity\n- **Multi-criteria grouping**: Groups by credibility threshold automatically\n\n**EntitiesTab:**\n- **Entity type cards**: 4-column grid (Person, Organization, Location, Event) with counts\n- **Grouped sections**: Collapsible sections for each entity type (People/Organizations expanded by default)\n- **Grid layout for lists**: 1-2 column responsive grid of entity cards\n- **Entity cards**: Show canonical name, aliases, mention count, descriptions\n\n**AnalysisTab:**\n- **Specialized cards per concept**: ContradictionCard, GapCard, CausalChainCard\n- **Visual metaphors**: Color-coded sections (red=contradiction, violet=gap, blue=chain)\n- **Structured comparison**: Side-by-side claims with \"VS\" separator\n- **Flow visualization**: Arrow indicators connecting causal steps\n\n**PerspectivesTab:**\n- **Comparison grid**: 3-column card grid showing perspective summaries (if multiple perspectives)\n- **Detailed cards**: Expandable perspective sections with full analysis\n- **Tri-column layout**: Key Insights | Warnings | Recommended Actions\n- **Type-based coloring**: Each perspective type has distinct color scheme (financial=emerald, investigative=rose, etc.)\n\n---\n\n### 3. Common Patterns Across Tabs\n\n**Component Reusability:**\n- **Section wrapper**: All tabs use the `<Section>` component for consistent card styling with optional collapsible behavior\n- **Badge system**: TypeBadge, ConfidenceBadge, CredibilityBadge, PriorityBadge used consistently\n- **Icons**: Centralized icon library (DocumentIcon, LinkIcon, AlertIcon, etc.) used throughout\n- **Empty states**: UnifiedEmptyState component for tabs with no data\n\n**UI/UX Conventions:**\n- **Color coding**: Semantic colors (emerald=good, amber=warning, red=bad, blue=info, violet=neutral)\n- **Progress bars**: ProgressBar component for confidence/credibility visualization\n- **Expandable content**: Chevron icons with rotation animation for expand/collapse\n- **Limiting displays**: Most tabs show 3-6 items by default with `.slice()` to prevent overwhelming UX\n\n**Data Processing:**\n- **Memoization**: useMemo hooks in FindingsTab and SourcesTab for efficient filtering\n- **Mapping structures**: sourceMap in FindingsTab for O(1) lookups\n- **Filtering logic**: Consistent filter pattern (search + category + confidence)\n\n**Layout Patterns:**\n- **Responsive grids**: `grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3` pattern\n- **Spacing**: Consistent `space-y-*` Tailwind utilities\n- **Borders and shadows**: Subtle shadows with `shadow-sm` and `border` classes\n\n---\n\n### 4. Gaps or Missing Functionality\n\n**Data Visualization Gaps:**\n1. **No interactive graphs**: Analysis of relationships could benefit from node/edge visualization\n2. **Missing timeline view**: Findings have temporal context but no visual timeline\n3. **No heat maps**: Contradiction/gap intensity not visualized\n4. **Limited source network**: Sources shown in table but no relationship view\n\n**Interaction/Filtering Gaps:**\n1. **No cross-tab filtering**: Selecting an entity doesn't filter findings to that entity\n2. **No export functionality**: No CSV/PDF download options visible\n3. **Missing sorting controls**: Tables don't show explicit sort options\n4. **No comparison mode**: Can't compare two perspectives side-by-side in detail\n\n**Analytics Gaps:**\n1. **No trend tracking**: Confidence scores over time not visible\n2. **Missing drill-down**: Can't navigate from overview to specific findings\n3. **No relationship view**: Finding relationships exist in data but aren't displayed\n4. **Limited source analysis**: No citation networks or source influence metrics\n\n**Content Gaps:**\n1. **No full source content**: SourcesTab shows snippets but not full article text\n2. **Missing entity profiles**: EntitiesTab cards lack detailed profile data (despite `profile_data` in schema)\n3. **No claim evidence trails**: Evidence connections not visualized\n4. **Limited causal explanation**: CausalChain shows steps but not reasoning\n\n---\n\n### 5. Opportunities for Enhancement\n\n**Tier 1: High-Impact UX Improvements**\n\n1. **Cross-Tab Navigation**\n   - Click entity in EntitiesTab to auto-filter FindingsTab\n   - Click finding to highlight related sources\n   - Click perspective to highlight findings analyzed\n\n2. **Advanced Filtering Dashboard**\n   - Multi-select filters that persist across sessions\n   - Date range picker for temporal filtering\n   - Confidence threshold slider\n   - Saved filter presets\n\n3. **Interactive Relationship Graph**\n   - Visualize claim relationships from RelationshipType\n   - Show entity-to-finding connections\n   - Highlight contradiction chains\n   - Drag-to-explore interaction model\n\n**Tier 2: Content Enrichment**\n\n4. **Enhanced Entity Profiles**\n   - Display profile_data fields from KnowledgeEntity\n   - Show external_ids (Wikipedia, etc.)\n   - Link to related entities\n   - Timeline of entity mentions across findings\n\n5. **Finding Details Panel**\n   - Deep dive modal showing related findings\n   - Knowledge claim lineage\n   - Corroboration analysis\n   - Alternative interpretations from different perspectives\n\n6. **Source Deep Dive**\n   - Full content viewer within tab\n   - Credibility factors breakdown (from credibility_factors data)\n   - Citation frequency visualization\n   - Source authority scoring explanation\n\n**Tier 3: Analytics & Insights**\n\n7. **Confidence Analytics**\n   - Confidence trend chart (do findings get more/less confident over time?)\n   - Confidence distribution across finding types\n   - Source impact on confidence scores\n\n8. **Contradiction Resolution View**\n   - Timeline of contradicting claims\n   - Source comparison for each side\n   - Evidence strength comparison\n   - Resolution suggestions interface\n\n9. **Gap Analysis Tools**\n   - Suggested query recommendations (already in data)\n   - Gap consequence analysis\n   - Research roadmap based on gaps\n   - Priority-based gap sequencing\n\n**Tier 4: Advanced Features**\n\n10. **Perspective Comparison Matrix**\n    - Side-by-side perspective comparison table\n    - Consensus/divergence analysis\n    - Confidence by perspective type\n    - Competing hypothesis visualization\n\n11. **Temporal Analysis**\n    - Timeline strip integrating findings and events\n    - Date range filters\n    - Event causality chains\n    - Chronological contradiction tracking\n\n12. **Quality Metrics Dashboard**\n    - Research completeness score\n    - Coverage analysis (topics, actors, timeframes)\n    - Credibility aggregate by category\n    - Data quality warnings\n\n**Tier 5: Data Extraction & Reporting**\n\n13. **Export Capabilities**\n    - Export filtered data as CSV/JSON\n    - Generate PDF reports with charts\n    - Create custom report templates\n    - Share specific tab views via links\n\n14. **Extracted Data Display**\n    - Better visualization of extracted_data fields\n    - Structured data table view\n    - Field validation and type indication\n    - Data quality flags\n\n---\n\n### 6. Architectural Improvements\n\n**Code Organization:**\n- Consider creating a tab navigation component at the parent level to manage state\n- Create custom hooks for common filtering logic (`useFilteredFindings`, `useFilteredSources`)\n- Extract color mapping objects to constants file (currently in component)\n\n**Performance Optimization:**\n- memoize expensive components with `React.memo()`\n- Consider virtual scrolling for large finding lists\n- Lazy load tab content when not visible\n- Implement pagination for large datasets instead of `.slice()`\n\n**Type Safety:**\n- Create derived types for filtered/grouped data\n- Type the perspective color mapping\n- Add prop validation with Zod/io-ts\n\n**State Management Considerations:**\n- Consider Zustand store for cross-tab filtering state\n- Session data caching strategy\n- Undo/redo for filter changes\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThe Report Tabs system is well-structured with clear separation of concerns and consistent UI patterns. The use of shared components (Section, badges, icons) ensures visual consistency, while memoization and filtering logic show performance awareness. However, the system is primarily **presentation-focused** rather than **interaction-focused**—tabs are largely independent, limiting the ability to perform sophisticated cross-tab analysis. The biggest opportunities lie in:\n\n1. **Connecting tabs** through cross-filtering and navigation\n2. **Visualizing relationships** that exist in the data but aren't currently displayed\n3. **Adding interactive analytics** for confidence and gap analysis\n4. **Enriching existing displays** with deeper data exploration capabilities\n\nThe type system (`SessionWithDetails`) already provides all necessary relationships and data; the tabs simply need more sophisticated visualization and interaction layers to leverage this information."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a26ca94 (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05d3e4f1-2259-4f3e-a055-8040a34c8b02","uuid":"d7dd2e67-2e5b-4938-92b0-0597462918ab","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Read and analyze these files to understand the Report Tabs system:\n- src/components/report/tabs/OverviewTab.tsx\n- src/components/report/tabs/FindingsTab.tsx\n- src/components/report/tabs/SourcesTab.tsx\n- src/components/report/tabs/EntitiesTab.tsx\n- src/components/report/tabs/AnalysisTab.tsx\n- src/components/report/tabs/PerspectivesTab.tsx\n- src/components/report/tabs/index.tsx\n\nProvide a comprehensive analysis of:\n1. Tab architecture and navigation patterns\n2. Data presentation approaches\n3. Common patterns across tabs\n4. Gaps or missing functionality\n5. Opportunities for enhancement","agentId":"a26ca94","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a comprehensive view of the Report Tabs system. Let me create a detailed analysis report.\n\n## Comprehensive Analysis of Report Tabs System\n\n### 1. Tab Architecture and Navigation Patterns\n\nThe Report Tabs system consists of 6 primary tabs organized around research intelligence analysis:\n\n**Tab Structure:**\n- **OverviewTab** - Dashboard view with key metrics and summaries\n- **FindingsTab** - Detailed findings with expandable cards and filtering\n- **SourcesTab** - Source credibility analysis with tabular presentation\n- **EntitiesTab** - Named entity extraction grouped by type\n- **AnalysisTab** - Contradictions, gaps, and causal chains\n- **PerspectivesTab** - Multi-perspective analytical views\n\n**Navigation Pattern:**\nAll tabs are exported from a centralized index file (`src/components/report/tabs/index.tsx`) which suggests a tabbed interface that likely switches between these components. The tabs appear to be designed for sequential navigation through research results, moving from high-level overview to detailed analysis.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Data Presentation Approaches\n\n**By Tab:**\n\n**OverviewTab:**\n- **Grid-based stats**: 5-column grid displaying key metrics (Findings, Avg Confidence, Sources, Perspectives, Red Flags)\n- **Metric cards** with color-coding and optional child visualizations\n- **Aggregated data**: Confidence distribution, warning summaries, key insights\n- **Limiting pattern**: Shows top 6 insights, 5 recommendations, 3 gaps (avoiding information overload)\n\n**FindingsTab:**\n- **Expandable cards**: Header shows summary with type/confidence badges; body reveals full content\n- **Hierarchical filtering**: Search, type filter, confidence filter (high/medium/low)\n- **Rich metadata display**: Temporal context, event date, extracted data, supporting sources\n- **Visual hierarchy**: Uses chevron icons and transitions for expand/collapse behavior\n\n**SourcesTab:**\n- **Credibility distribution bar**: Visual breakdown of source quality tiers\n- **Tabular layout**: Rows with source title, type badge, credibility progress bar, and date\n- **Hover states**: Light background change for interactivity\n- **Multi-criteria grouping**: Groups by credibility threshold automatically\n\n**EntitiesTab:**\n- **Entity type cards**: 4-column grid (Person, Organization, Location, Event) with counts\n- **Grouped sections**: Collapsible sections for each entity type (People/Organizations expanded by default)\n- **Grid layout for lists**: 1-2 column responsive grid of entity cards\n- **Entity cards**: Show canonical name, aliases, mention count, descriptions\n\n**AnalysisTab:**\n- **Specialized cards per concept**: ContradictionCard, GapCard, CausalChainCard\n- **Visual metaphors**: Color-coded sections (red=contradiction, violet=gap, blue=chain)\n- **Structured comparison**: Side-by-side claims with \"VS\" separator\n- **Flow visualization**: Arrow indicators connecting causal steps\n\n**PerspectivesTab:**\n- **Comparison grid**: 3-column card grid showing perspective summaries (if multiple perspectives)\n- **Detailed cards**: Expandable perspective sections with full analysis\n- **Tri-column layout**: Key Insights | Warnings | Recommended Actions\n- **Type-based coloring**: Each perspective type has distinct color scheme (financial=emerald, investigative=rose, etc.)\n\n---\n\n### 3. Common Patterns Across Tabs\n\n**Component Reusability:**\n- **Section wrapper**: All tabs use the `<Section>` component for consistent card styling with optional collapsible behavior\n- **Badge system**: TypeBadge, ConfidenceBadge, CredibilityBadge, PriorityBadge used consistently\n- **Icons**: Centralized icon library (DocumentIcon, LinkIcon, AlertIcon, etc.) used throughout\n- **Empty states**: UnifiedEmptyState component for tabs with no data\n\n**UI/UX Conventions:**\n- **Color coding**: Semantic colors (emerald=good, amber=warning, red=bad, blue=info, violet=neutral)\n- **Progress bars**: ProgressBar component for confidence/credibility visualization\n- **Expandable content**: Chevron icons with rotation animation for expand/collapse\n- **Limiting displays**: Most tabs show 3-6 items by default with `.slice()` to prevent overwhelming UX\n\n**Data Processing:**\n- **Memoization**: useMemo hooks in FindingsTab and SourcesTab for efficient filtering\n- **Mapping structures**: sourceMap in FindingsTab for O(1) lookups\n- **Filtering logic**: Consistent filter pattern (search + category + confidence)\n\n**Layout Patterns:**\n- **Responsive grids**: `grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3` pattern\n- **Spacing**: Consistent `space-y-*` Tailwind utilities\n- **Borders and shadows**: Subtle shadows with `shadow-sm` and `border` classes\n\n---\n\n### 4. Gaps or Missing Functionality\n\n**Data Visualization Gaps:**\n1. **No interactive graphs**: Analysis of relationships could benefit from node/edge visualization\n2. **Missing timeline view**: Findings have temporal context but no visual timeline\n3. **No heat maps**: Contradiction/gap intensity not visualized\n4. **Limited source network**: Sources shown in table but no relationship view\n\n**Interaction/Filtering Gaps:**\n1. **No cross-tab filtering**: Selecting an entity doesn't filter findings to that entity\n2. **No export functionality**: No CSV/PDF download options visible\n3. **Missing sorting controls**: Tables don't show explicit sort options\n4. **No comparison mode**: Can't compare two perspectives side-by-side in detail\n\n**Analytics Gaps:**\n1. **No trend tracking**: Confidence scores over time not visible\n2. **Missing drill-down**: Can't navigate from overview to specific findings\n3. **No relationship view**: Finding relationships exist in data but aren't displayed\n4. **Limited source analysis**: No citation networks or source influence metrics\n\n**Content Gaps:**\n1. **No full source content**: SourcesTab shows snippets but not full article text\n2. **Missing entity profiles**: EntitiesTab cards lack detailed profile data (despite `profile_data` in schema)\n3. **No claim evidence trails**: Evidence connections not visualized\n4. **Limited causal explanation**: CausalChain shows steps but not reasoning\n\n---\n\n### 5. Opportunities for Enhancement\n\n**Tier 1: High-Impact UX Improvements**\n\n1. **Cross-Tab Navigation**\n   - Click entity in EntitiesTab to auto-filter FindingsTab\n   - Click finding to highlight related sources\n   - Click perspective to highlight findings analyzed\n\n2. **Advanced Filtering Dashboard**\n   - Multi-select filters that persist across sessions\n   - Date range picker for temporal filtering\n   - Confidence threshold slider\n   - Saved filter presets\n\n3. **Interactive Relationship Graph**\n   - Visualize claim relationships from RelationshipType\n   - Show entity-to-finding connections\n   - Highlight contradiction chains\n   - Drag-to-explore interaction model\n\n**Tier 2: Content Enrichment**\n\n4. **Enhanced Entity Profiles**\n   - Display profile_data fields from KnowledgeEntity\n   - Show external_ids (Wikipedia, etc.)\n   - Link to related entities\n   - Timeline of entity mentions across findings\n\n5. **Finding Details Panel**\n   - Deep dive modal showing related findings\n   - Knowledge claim lineage\n   - Corroboration analysis\n   - Alternative interpretations from different perspectives\n\n6. **Source Deep Dive**\n   - Full content viewer within tab\n   - Credibility factors breakdown (from credibility_factors data)\n   - Citation frequency visualization\n   - Source authority scoring explanation\n\n**Tier 3: Analytics & Insights**\n\n7. **Confidence Analytics**\n   - Confidence trend chart (do findings get more/less confident over time?)\n   - Confidence distribution across finding types\n   - Source impact on confidence scores\n\n8. **Contradiction Resolution View**\n   - Timeline of contradicting claims\n   - Source comparison for each side\n   - Evidence strength comparison\n   - Resolution suggestions interface\n\n9. **Gap Analysis Tools**\n   - Suggested query recommendations (already in data)\n   - Gap consequence analysis\n   - Research roadmap based on gaps\n   - Priority-based gap sequencing\n\n**Tier 4: Advanced Features**\n\n10. **Perspective Comparison Matrix**\n    - Side-by-side perspective comparison table\n    - Consensus/divergence analysis\n    - Confidence by perspective type\n    - Competing hypothesis visualization\n\n11. **Temporal Analysis**\n    - Timeline strip integrating findings and events\n    - Date range filters\n    - Event causality chains\n    - Chronological contradiction tracking\n\n12. **Quality Metrics Dashboard**\n    - Research completeness score\n    - Coverage analysis (topics, actors, timeframes)\n    - Credibility aggregate by category\n    - Data quality warnings\n\n**Tier 5: Data Extraction & Reporting**\n\n13. **Export Capabilities**\n    - Export filtered data as CSV/JSON\n    - Generate PDF reports with charts\n    - Create custom report templates\n    - Share specific tab views via links\n\n14. **Extracted Data Display**\n    - Better visualization of extracted_data fields\n    - Structured data table view\n    - Field validation and type indication\n    - Data quality flags\n\n---\n\n### 6. Architectural Improvements\n\n**Code Organization:**\n- Consider creating a tab navigation component at the parent level to manage state\n- Create custom hooks for common filtering logic (`useFilteredFindings`, `useFilteredSources`)\n- Extract color mapping objects to constants file (currently in component)\n\n**Performance Optimization:**\n- memoize expensive components with `React.memo()`\n- Consider virtual scrolling for large finding lists\n- Lazy load tab content when not visible\n- Implement pagination for large datasets instead of `.slice()`\n\n**Type Safety:**\n- Create derived types for filtered/grouped data\n- Type the perspective color mapping\n- Add prop validation with Zod/io-ts\n\n**State Management Considerations:**\n- Consider Zustand store for cross-tab filtering state\n- Session data caching strategy\n- Undo/redo for filter changes\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThe Report Tabs system is well-structured with clear separation of concerns and consistent UI patterns. The use of shared components (Section, badges, icons) ensures visual consistency, while memoization and filtering logic show performance awareness. However, the system is primarily **presentation-focused** rather than **interaction-focused**—tabs are largely independent, limiting the ability to perform sophisticated cross-tab analysis. The biggest opportunities lie in:\n\n1. **Connecting tabs** through cross-filtering and navigation\n2. **Visualizing relationships** that exist in the data but aren't currently displayed\n3. **Adding interactive analytics** for confidence and gap analysis\n4. **Enriching existing displays** with deeper data exploration capabilities\n\nThe type system (`SessionWithDetails`) already provides all necessary relationships and data; the tabs simply need more sophisticated visualization and interaction layers to leverage this information."}],"totalDurationMs":152959,"totalTokens":39058,"totalToolUseCount":11,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9639,"cache_read_input_tokens":29411,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9639},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"}}}
[2026-01-29T23:34:53.626Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ffk4p85VqsrZh3VbgUUzfh","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→import { create } from 'zustand';\n     2→import type { ResearchSession, SessionWithDetails } from '@/src/types/research';\n     3→import { MOCK_SESSIONS, USE_MOCK_DATA } from '@/src/lib/mockData';\n     4→\n     5→// ============================================\n     6→// APP STATE - Unified store for Research Map\n     7→// ============================================\n     8→\n     9→// Topic type with sessions for intermediate categorization\n    10→export interface TopicWithSessions {\n    11→  id: string;\n    12→  name: string;\n    13→  slug: string;\n    14→  description?: string;\n    15→  topic_type?: string;\n    16→  parent_id?: string;\n    17→  session_count: number;\n    18→  finding_count: number;\n    19→  entity_count: number;\n    20→  sessions: Array<{\n    21→    id: string;\n    22→    title: string;\n    23→    template_type: string;\n    24→    status: string;\n    25→    claim_count: number;\n    26→    source_count: number;\n    27→  }>;\n    28→  children?: TopicWithSessions[];\n    29→}\n    30→\n    31→interface AppState {\n    32→  // Sessions data (for map visualization)\n    33→  sessions: ResearchSession[];\n    34→  sessionsLoading: boolean;\n    35→  sessionsError: string | null;\n    36→\n    37→  // Topics data (for intermediate categorization)\n    38→  topics: TopicWithSessions[];\n    39→  topicsLoading: boolean;\n    40→  topicsError: string | null;\n    41→\n    42→  // Current selected session (for report modal)\n    43→  currentSession: SessionWithDetails | null;\n    44→  currentSessionLoading: boolean;\n    45→  currentSessionError: string | null;\n    46→  isReportModalOpen: boolean;\n    47→\n    48→  // Map navigation state\n    49→  mapZoomPath: string[]; // Breadcrumb path: ['root', 'financial', 'topic-1']\n    50→  mapSelectedNode: string | null;\n    51→\n    52→  // Actions\n    53→  fetchSessions: () => Promise<void>;\n    54→  fetchTopics: () => Promise<void>;\n    55→  fetchSession: (id: string) => Promise<void>;\n    56→  setMapZoomPath: (path: string[]) => void;\n    57→  setMapSelectedNode: (nodeId: string | null) => void;\n    58→  openReportModal: (sessionId: string) => Promise<void>;\n    59→  closeReportModal: () => void;\n    60→  clearCurrentSession: () => void;\n    61→}\n    62→\n    63→export const useAppStore = create<AppState>((set, get) => ({\n    64→  // Initial state\n    65→  sessions: [],\n    66→  sessionsLoading: false,\n    67→  sessionsError: null,\n    68→\n    69→  topics: [],\n    70→  topicsLoading: false,\n    71→  topicsError: null,\n    72→\n    73→  currentSession: null,\n    74→  currentSessionLoading: false,\n    75→  currentSessionError: null,\n    76→  isReportModalOpen: false,\n    77→\n    78→  mapZoomPath: ['root'],\n    79→  mapSelectedNode: null,\n    80→\n    81→  // Fetch all sessions for the map via API route\n    82→  fetchSessions: async () => {\n    83→    set({ sessionsLoading: true, sessionsError: null });\n    84→\n    85→    try {\n    86→      // Use mock data if explicitly enabled\n    87→      if (USE_MOCK_DATA) {\n    88→        set({ sessions: MOCK_SESSIONS, sessionsLoading: false });\n    89→        return;\n    90→      }\n    91→\n    92→      const response = await fetch('/api/sessions');\n    93→\n    94→      if (!response.ok) {\n    95→        throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}: ${response.statusText}`);\n    96→      }\n    97→\n    98→      const data = await response.json();\n    99→      const sessions: ResearchSession[] = data.sessions || [];\n   100→\n   101→      // Fall back to mock data if no real sessions\n   102→      if (sessions.length === 0) {\n   103→        console.log('No real sessions found, using mock data for demo');\n   104→        set({ sessions: MOCK_SESSIONS, sessionsLoading: false });\n   105→        return;\n   106→      }\n   107→\n   108→      set({ sessions, sessionsLoading: false });\n   109→    } catch (error) {\n   110→      console.error('Error fetching sessions:', error);\n   111→      // Fall back to mock data on error for demo purposes\n   112→      console.log('Database error, using mock data for demo');\n   113→      set({ sessions: MOCK_SESSIONS, sessionsLoading: false });\n   114→    }\n   115→  },\n   116→\n   117→  // Fetch all topics for intermediate categorization\n   118→  fetchTopics: async () => {\n   119→    set({ topicsLoading: true, topicsError: null });\n   120→\n   121→    try {\n   122→      const response = await fetch('/api/topics');\n   123→\n   124→      if (!response.ok) {\n   125→        throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}: ${response.statusText}`);\n   126→      }\n   127→\n   128→      const data = await response.json();\n   129→      const topics: TopicWithSessions[] = data.topics || [];\n   130→\n   131→      set({ topics, topicsLoading: false });\n   132→    } catch (error) {\n   133→      console.error('Error fetching topics:', error);\n   134→      set({\n   135→        topicsError: error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Failed to fetch topics',\n   136→        topicsLoading: false,\n   137→        topics: [],\n   138→      });\n   139→    }\n   140→  },\n   141→\n   142→  // Fetch a single session with full details via API route\n   143→  fetchSession: async (id: string) => {\n   144→    set({ currentSessionLoading: true, currentSessionError: null });\n   145→\n   146→    try {\n   147→      const response = await fetch(`/api/sessions/${id}`);\n   148→\n   149→      if (!response.ok) {\n   150→        if (response.status === 404) {\n   151→          set({\n   152→            currentSessionError: 'Session not found',\n   153→            currentSessionLoading: false,\n   154→          });\n   155→          return;\n   156→        }\n   157→        throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}: ${response.statusText}`);\n   158→      }\n   159→\n   160→      const session: SessionWithDetails = await response.json();\n   161→      set({ currentSession: session, currentSessionLoading: false });\n   162→    } catch (error) {\n   163→      console.error('Error fetching session:', error);\n   164→      set({\n   165→        currentSessionError: error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Failed to fetch session',\n   166→        currentSessionLoading: false,\n   167→      });\n   168→    }\n   169→  },\n   170→\n   171→  // Map navigation\n   172→  setMapZoomPath: (path: string[]) => {\n   173→    set({ mapZoomPath: path });\n   174→  },\n   175→\n   176→  setMapSelectedNode: (nodeId: string | null) => {\n   177→    set({ mapSelectedNode: nodeId });\n   178→  },\n   179→\n   180→  // Report modal\n   181→  openReportModal: async (sessionId: string) => {\n   182→    set({ isReportModalOpen: true, currentSessionLoading: true });\n   183→    await get().fetchSession(sessionId);\n   184→  },\n   185→\n   186→  closeReportModal: () => {\n   187→    set({ isReportModalOpen: false });\n   188→  },\n   189→\n   190→  clearCurrentSession: () => {\n   191→    set({\n   192→      currentSession: null,\n   193→      currentSessionError: null,\n   194→      isReportModalOpen: false,\n   195→    });\n   196→  },\n   197→}));\n   198→\n   199→// ============================================\n   200→// DERIVED DATA HELPERS\n   201→// ============================================\n   202→\n   203→// Group sessions by template type\n   204→export function groupSessionsByTemplate(sessions: ResearchSession[]): Record<string, ResearchSession[]> {\n   205→  return sessions.reduce((acc, session) => {\n   206→    const template = session.template_type || 'unknown';\n   207→    if (!acc[template]) {\n   208→      acc[template] = [];\n   209→    }\n   210→    acc[template].push(session);\n   211→    return acc;\n   212→  }, {} as Record<string, ResearchSession[]>);\n   213→}\n   214→\n   215→// Get template display name\n   216→export function getTemplateDisplayName(template: string): string {\n   217→  const displayNames: Record<string, string> = {\n   218→    investigative: 'Investigative',\n   219→    financial: 'Financial',\n   220→    competitive: 'Competitive',\n   221→    tech_market: 'Tech Market',\n   222→    legal: 'Legal',\n   223→    due_diligence: 'Due Diligence',\n   224→    contract: 'Contract',\n   225→    purchase_decision: 'Purchase Decision',\n   226→    reputation: 'Reputation',\n   227→    understanding: 'Understanding',\n   228→  };\n   229→  return displayNames[template] || template.replace(/_/g, ' ');\n   230→}\n   231→\n   232→// Get template color\n   233→export function getTemplateColor(template: string): string {\n   234→  const colors: Record<string, string> = {\n   235→    investigative: '#E03131',\n   236→    financial: '#228BE6',\n   237→    competitive: '#7950F2',\n   238→    tech_market: '#2F9E44',\n   239→    legal: '#1864AB',\n   240→    due_diligence: '#F59F00',\n   241→    contract: '#862E9C',\n   242→    purchase_decision: '#E8590C',\n   243→    reputation: '#C2255C',\n   244→    understanding: '#0B7285',\n   245→  };\n   246→  return colors[template] || '#8A8987';\n   247→}\n   248→\n   249→// Extract topic from query (simple heuristic)\n   250→export function extractTopicFromQuery(query: string): string {\n   251→  // Remove common research prefixes\n   252→  const cleaned = query\n   253→    .replace(/^(research|analyze|investigate|understand|compare)\\s+/i, '')\n   254→    .replace(/\\s+(market|analysis|report|overview)$/i, '');\n   255→\n   256→  // Truncate to reasonable length\n   257→  if (cleaned.length > 50) {\n   258→    return cleaned.substring(0, 47) + '...';\n   259→  }\n   260→  return cleaned;\n   261→}\n   262→\n   263→// Calculate statistics for sessions\n   264→export function calculateSessionStats(sessions: ResearchSession[]) {\n   265→  const totalSessions = sessions.length;\n   266→  const totalFindings = sessions.reduce((sum, s) => sum + (s.claim_count || 0), 0);\n   267→  const totalSources = sessions.reduce((sum, s) => sum + (s.source_count || 0), 0);\n   268→  const completedSessions = sessions.filter(s => s.status === 'completed').length;\n   269→\n   270→  const byTemplate = groupSessionsByTemplate(sessions);\n   271→  const templateCounts = Object.entries(byTemplate).map(([template, items]) => ({\n   272→    template,\n   273→    count: items.length,\n   274→    color: getTemplateColor(template),\n   275→  }));\n   276→\n   277→  return {\n   278→    totalSessions,\n   279→    totalFindings,\n   280→    totalSources,\n   281→    completedSessions,\n   282→    templateCounts,\n   283→  };\n   284→}\n   285→\n   286→// ============================================\n   287→// TOPIC-BASED GROUPING HELPERS\n   288→// ============================================\n   289→\n   290→// Group sessions by topic within a template\n   291→export function groupSessionsByTopic(\n   292→  sessions: ResearchSession[],\n   293→  topics: TopicWithSessions[]\n   294→): Record<string, { topic: TopicWithSessions; sessions: ResearchSession[] }> {\n   295→  const result: Record<string, { topic: TopicWithSessions; sessions: ResearchSession[] }> = {};\n   296→\n   297→  // Create a map of session_id to topic\n   298→  const sessionToTopic = new Map<string, TopicWithSessions>();\n   299→  topics.forEach(topic => {\n   300→    topic.sessions?.forEach(s => {\n   301→      sessionToTopic.set(s.id, topic);\n   302→    });\n   303→  });\n   304→\n   305→  // Group sessions\n   306→  sessions.forEach(session => {\n   307→    const topic = sessionToTopic.get(session.id);\n   308→    if (topic) {\n   309→      if (!result[topic.id]) {\n   310→        result[topic.id] = { topic, sessions: [] };\n   311→      }\n   312→      result[topic.id].sessions.push(session);\n   313→    }\n   314→  });\n   315→\n   316→  return result;\n   317→}\n   318→\n   319→// Get topics that belong to a specific template\n   320→export function getTopicsForTemplate(\n   321→  template: string,\n   322→  topics: TopicWithSessions[]\n   323→): TopicWithSessions[] {\n   324→  return topics.filter(topic =>\n   325→    topic.sessions?.some(s => s.template_type === template)\n   326→  );\n   327→}\n   328→\n   329→// Get sessions without a topic (uncategorized)\n   330→export function getUncategorizedSessions(\n   331→  sessions: ResearchSession[],\n   332→  topics: TopicWithSessions[]\n   333→): ResearchSession[] {\n   334→  const categorizedIds = new Set<string>();\n   335→  topics.forEach(topic => {\n   336→    topic.sessions?.forEach(s => categorizedIds.add(s.id));\n   337→  });\n   338→\n   339→  return sessions.filter(s => !categorizedIds.has(s.id));\n   340→}\n   341→\n   342→// Get finding type counts for display\n   343→export function getFindingTypeIcon(type: string): string {\n   344→  const icons: Record<string, string> = {\n   345→    fact: 'check-circle',\n   346→    claim: 'message-circle',\n   347→    event: 'calendar',\n   348→    actor: 'user',\n   349→    relationship: 'link',\n   350→    pattern: 'trending-up',\n   351→    gap: 'alert-circle',\n   352→    evidence: 'file-text',\n   353→  };\n   354→  return icons[type] || 'circle';\n   355→}\n   356→\n   357→export function getFindingTypeColor(type: string): string {\n   358→  const colors: Record<string, string> = {\n   359→    fact: '#2563EB',\n   360→    claim: '#7C3AED',\n   361→    event: '#059669',\n   362→    actor: '#DC2626',\n   363→    relationship: '#D97706',\n   364→    pattern: '#0891B2',\n   365→    gap: '#6B7280',\n   366→    evidence: '#16A34A',\n   367→  };\n   368→  return colors[type] || '#6B7280';\n   369→}\n   370→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SLeQqT8ZZ6fBnLfDVasWDD","session_id":"05d3e4f1-2259-4f3e-a055-8040a34c8b02","uuid":"e5f6d84a-9b92-46f3-b7b0-7a3eaa5bd142"}
